body:
So I found the source of a long standing issue in http://Clara.io which of course uses Three.JS.  It isn't yet fixed in production, but I know where it is coming from.

Basically `Euler.setFromQuaternion()` produces the wrong result on about 0.1% of possible quaternion values.  When it screws up it disagrees with the result of creating a `Euler` from a `Quaternion` via an intermediate `Matrix4`.

Here is an example that shows the disagreement on a problematic quaternion value.  Normally there is no disagreement between these two conversion routes from quaternion to euler, but if they do disagree, the eViaQ1 conversion method is wrong, while the eViaMViaQ1 conversion method is correct.

This bug has been around a long time and it is in the most recent version of Three.JS as well.

```
var eulerOrder = "ZYX";

// known problematic quaternions
var q1 = new THREE.Quaternion( 0.5207769385244341, -0.4783214164122354, 0.520776938524434, 0.47832141641223547 );
var q2 = new THREE.Quaternion( 0.11284905712620674, 0.6980437630368944, -0.11284905712620674, 0.6980437630368944 );

// create Euler directly from a Quaternion
var eViaQ1 = new THREE.Euler().setFromQuaternion( q1, eulerOrder ); // there is likely a bug here

// create Euler from Quaternion via an intermediate Matrix4
var mViaQ1 = new THREE.Matrix4().makeRotationFromQuaternion( q1 );
var eViaMViaQ1 = new THREE.Euler().setFromRotationMatrix( mViaQ1, eulerOrder );

// the results here are different
console.log( eViaQ1 );  // this result is wrong
console.log( eViaMViaQ1 );  // this result is correct
```

I will likely investigate this further tomorrow.
